WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:06.470
Have you ever wondered what Pokémon might
look like as realistic creatures?

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:12.100
By their very nature, Pokémon are highly
stylized, so reimagining them as more biologically

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.460
feasible lifeforms presents an exciting challenge.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:21.490
This concept is the idea behind the Realistic
Pokémon project, a series by RJ Palmer, aka

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:26.160
‘Arvalis’ who is one of my favorite creature
designers, and is so talented he was hired

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.369
to assist with the designs for movies like
Detective Pikachu.

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:33.239
I have links to where you can follow and support
him in this video’s description.

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:38.210
His art imagines what kind of biology a more
scientifically grounded version of a Pokémon

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.870
might have — often with fascinating results.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:46.699
So, for this entry into the archive, you can
expect a Pokémon nature documentary of sorts,

00:00:46.699 --> 00:00:50.179
examining Palmer’s redesigned Pokémon as
if they were real animals.

00:00:50.179 --> 00:00:55.379
Before we get started, a quick disclaimer:
These realistic lifeforms are an awesome speculative

00:00:55.379 --> 00:01:00.469
exercise, but not an attempt to somehow ‘improve’
the stylized designs of the original games.

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:03.370
They’re just meant to be interesting alternate
takes.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:08.770
Also, when Palmer calls these designs ‘realistic,’
he means they ground the appearance of Pokémon

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:13.360
in real-world anatomy — but many are of
course still more fantastical than what you’d

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.250
find in nature, and in some cases still possess
their elemental abilities… because it’s

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.460
more exciting that way.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.390
He incorporates real science and biology when
he can, but the main goal of this project

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:24.760
is just to have fun.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.970
With that said, let’s get started, and dive
into the incredible world of realistic Pokémon…

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:35.110
We’ll begin our journey through the natural
world of Pokémon in the Kanto region, and

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.670
examine realistic versions of the starter
lines.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:42.391
Opening with Venusaur and its pre-evolutions
— you can see how different Pokémon look

00:01:42.391 --> 00:01:45.009
when rendered with more realistic detail.

00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:50.020
Here, Palmer has molded the fantastical anatomy
of the Venusaur line off real animals like

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.979
horned lizards and the extinct parareptile
genus Scutosaurus — an organism that wasn’t

00:01:54.979 --> 00:01:57.680
actually that far off in size from a Venusaur.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.590
Palmer also imagines the organisms have an
endosymbiotic relationship with a large type

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:08.039
of flower — similar in size to real life
Rafflesia plants which can reach four feet,

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.959
or 1.2 meters in length.

00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:14.819
Actual Rafflesia are also parasitic, meaning
they derive some of their nutritional requirements

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:16.180
from other plants.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:21.099
We can imagine our Venusaurs have a similar
(albeit more mutually beneficial) arrangement

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:25.030
with the plant that grows from their backs,
giving the flowers energy in exchange for

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.840
added defense, and perhaps vice versa.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.840
Plant-animal symbiosis isn’t completely
unheard of in nature — like hummingbirds

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.530
pollinating flowers, or moss growing on the
fur of sloths (although sloths don’t have

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.599
much say in that one).

00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:42.450
This Venusaur line is definitely taking this
to a new level, but it’s more fun to imagine

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.530
a lifeform with a giant symbiotic plant than
without one.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:51.580
Charizard and its pre-evolutions are up next,
and have been reimagined in fearsome detail.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.120
Palmer has added new complexities of scale
patterns, ridges, and layers of muscle to

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.569
help ground these more fantastical creatures
in the natural world.

00:02:59.569 --> 00:03:03.870
For inspiration, Palmer has drawn from the
appearance of many different types of real-world

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:08.040
reptiles — some of which look like they
could be from another world if you stare at

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.340
them long enough.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.819
The claws on the forelimbs in particular remind
me of the anatomy of monitor lizards like

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:18.450
the Komodo dragon, which in real life can
grow up to 10 ft, or 3 meters.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:23.920
And like most monitor lizards, this more biologically
feasible version of Charizard usually stays

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.319
lower to the ground due to its weight, and
is rearing up in this image as an intimidation

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:29.319
display.

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:33.760
And the last starter we’ll be covering here
is Blastoise and its pre-evolutions.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.590
These turtle-like creatures now seem much
better adapted for life in an aquatic environment,

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:42.700
with sea turtle biology forming the basis
for all three designs.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:47.569
Palmer imagines Squirtle might have long,
rudder like tails to steer through the water

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:51.630
— and much like a baby sea turtle, are quite
a bit slower on land.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.180
In contrast, Wartortle — seen here having
a minor standoff with a quite realistic Krabby

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.129
— spend much of their time resting on the
seafloor.

00:03:59.129 --> 00:04:03.870
Palmer has depicted Wartortle’s head-fins
as a unique kind of fan coral that grow from

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.280
the creature’s head due to their sedentary
lifestyle.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.950
And the aquatic behemoth Blastoise is perhaps
the trickiest to get right.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:16.019
Cleverly, Palmer has reimagined its cannons
as biological water jets much like the ones

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:21.070
some real-world squid species use for propulsion,
although Blastoise’s jets have evolved to

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.630
be much more powerful.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:27.140
If you look closely, you can also see Staryu
and Shellder in this image —which creates

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:31.050
an immersive sense of a rich underwater ecosystem.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:36.390
Moving past starters, interestingly, Palmer
reimagines Dragonite as a giant amphibian,

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:40.820
taking inspiration from the Japanese Giant
Salamander — and basing Dragonite’s earlier

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:44.700
forms Dratini and Dragonair off salamander
tadpoles.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:49.250
In nature, some salamander tadpoles do feature
frill-like structures on the sides of their

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:53.030
head, which do look somewhat like Dratini’s
little side-crests.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:57.310
Strange though it might sound, in the real
world, these body parts are actually the salamander’s

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:58.730
external gills.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.960
Since salamanders are one of the few real-world
animals where a serpentine-looking creature

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.910
transforms into a reptilian-looking critter,
it’s a pretty clever fit for a more biologically

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.090
feasible dragonite.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:15.300
And while real world Japanese Giant Salamanders
of course aren’t dragons, they might be

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:16.390
the next closest thing.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:17.390
I could really see this sort of creature living
in swampy environments.

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:21.080
Up next, we have various evolutions of the
unique Pokémon Eevee.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:25.860
In this more realistic world, Palmer imagines
these organisms are the result of a single

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:29.510
species diversifying to adapt to different
environments.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:34.100
There are all sorts of small creative details
here that make this image great, like Vaporeon

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:39.710
having more defined flipper-like feet — almost
like a seal or sea lion — Espeon resembling

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:44.740
a hairless sphynx cat, and Leafeon having
a high density of moss in their fur, giving

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.770
them the appearance of being green – sort
of like a sloth.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:54.240
In the category of Legendary Pokémon, Palmer
imagines Arcanine as a powerful predator,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.000
taking anatomical inspiration from canines,
of course, but also borrowing features from

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:04.700
lions and tigers, which makes sense considering
Arcanine in the games has something of a lion’s

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.570
mane and some tiger-like markings.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:12.430
Plus, if any real animals deserve the title
of ‘Legendary,’ lions and tigers are definitely

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:17.230
among them, so it makes sense that a more
lifelike Arcanine would resemble them.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:21.970
Palmer also drew the pre-evolved form Growlithe,
which I think looks a bit like a lion cub.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:27.410
A small detail I like is it seems to be playing
with a turtwig, although the turtwig doesn’t

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:28.690
seem too happy about it.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:33.930
For the Pokémon Nidoking, Palmer took inspiration
from various sources, adding the leathery

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:39.690
armor and central horn of a rhino, and also
borrowing the claws and spines of a porcupine

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.160
for the rest of the anatomy.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.740
The result is a truly unique looking creature.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.460
And near this Pokémon’s tail, Palmer snuck
in a realistic interpretation of a Pikachu,

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:51.340
which not surprisingly looks quite rodent-like.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:56.780
In the realm of flying-types, Palmer has converted
Honchkrow into much more biologically-feasible

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:01.970
looking creatures— an impressive feat, considering
how stylized Honchkrow are in the games.

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:06.060
Their ‘beards’ have been reimagined as
parts of their body they can puff up to attract

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.590
mates, similar to the displays of some birds
of paradise.

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:14.560
Their hats have also been reimagined as unique-looking
bony plates — which are actually quite similar

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:19.560
to the bony plates on the heads of real-life
cassowaries, plates that cassowaries can use

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.420
as a deceptively powerful weapon.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:27.340
Another challenging Pokémon to make realistic
is Garchomp, who happens to be one of my personal

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:28.340
favorites.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:34.240
This version is an imposing carnivore, imagined
as a fusion of a large theropod dinosaur and

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.700
some kind of shark… just on land.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:42.710
In fact, I could imagine this strange predator
stalking the sands of an arid environment.

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:47.290
Definitely one Pokémon which might be dangerous
to encounter in nature.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:53.140
Another dinosaur-like Pokémon, but with some
intriguing insect influences, is Scyther — depicted

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:56.390
here looking quite different from its original
design.

00:07:56.390 --> 00:08:00.030
The creature’s forearms in this version
resemble those of a Mantis, although this

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:04.850
reinterpretation also has some pretty clear
theropod influences as well.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:09.520
Its wings are depicted as a kind of back sail
— like a thinner version of the sails on

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.770
the backs of dinosaurs like Spinosaurus.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.800
A pretty inventive reimagining.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:14.800
And rendered in this much realistic detail,
it once again really hits home how dangerous

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:15.800
some Pokémon might be if they were real-world
wildlife.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.950
Perhaps the trickiest Pokémon on this list
to imagine as a living organism is Gengar

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.720
and its pre-evolutions.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:27.110
Instead of ghosts, this evolutionary line
has been reimagined as bat-like organisms:

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:29.050
which fits their spooky theme.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:33.740
Starting with Gastly, Palmer depicts this
bizarre pre-evolution as a poisonous bat with

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:38.800
a specialized helium bladder that allows it
to float, almost like a living balloon.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:39.800
Strange?

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:44.940
Sure, but you’ve got to get creative when
imagining a ghost type as a wild animal.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:49.560
Haunters have been realized with translucent
wings to give them that floating hand look,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.630
which is a creative way to ground the design
biologically.

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:58.140
And finally, Gengar is a heavier, terrestrial
creature, which Palmer imagines as an active

00:08:58.140 --> 00:08:59.190
carnivore.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:03.620
Zoroark has also been given the carnivore
treatment, with its anatomy now much closer

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:08.460
to canine predator, and the stylized elements
of the original design toned down.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:13.270
In the skull and teeth region, Palmer’s
Zoroark somewhat resembles a hyena.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:17.580
And moving down to the ground, also ready
for their closeup, is Diglet, conceptualized

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:21.850
as a more conventional burrowing lifeform
akin to a star-nosed mole.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:25.070
Which is good, because star-nosed moles are
the best.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:30.260
Returning at last to Legendary Pokémon, this
awe-inspiring reimagining of Reshiram focuses

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:35.310
on the avian qualities of the Legendary to
create a more taxonomically consistent design.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:40.230
An ancient and powerful being in the games,
this portrayal likewise takes inspiration

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:45.530
from the anatomy of proto-birds and early
avian dinosaurs — just scaled up to epic

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:46.680
proportions.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:51.450
One extra worldbuilding detail is that Palmer
imagines the silver bangles on its neck were

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:55.560
offerings from early humans who worshiped
it like a deity.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:00.780
And as the finale of our journey through realistic
Pokémon designs, we have Groudon — looking

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:05.070
straight out of an epic myth when rendered
in realistic detail.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:10.270
Huge and pulsing with some unknown bioluminescent
display, this gargantuan creature stretches

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:15.460
the limits of size for wildlife — taking
anatomical cues from the largest of theropod

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:16.460
dinosaurs.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:20.640
It’s the sort of behemoth that would seem
right at home stomping through a volcanic

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:21.700
landscape.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:25.570
Of course, a creature of this size would have
an effect on its environment.

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:30.130
Running from this titan’s thundering steps
is a Magmar, which in this interpretation

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:37.020
resembles a shoe-billed stork — an animal
that proves nature can frankly be just as

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:39.070
weird as Pokémon.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:43.740
The world of realistic Pokémon is vast and
has yet to be fully explored, but we’ve

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.010
reached the end for this particular video.

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:50.550
There are extra designs on RJ Palmer’s site
and in his artbooks, so check the links below

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:51.560
if you’re interested.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.650
And I’ll be back next week with Part II,
which will feature Palmer’s more recent

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:58.010
designs—where things get even wilder.

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:00.170
Also, thanks for watching.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:04.860
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.820
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:08.990
See you in the next video.

